      "content": "That's much clearer, thank you. I'll focus on the heteroscedasticity tests you mentioned to see if there's a pattern in the variance.\n\nOne thing that's still bothering me is the sheer \"presence\" of these errors. When I look at the residual plots, it doesn't just look like mathematical noise; it feels almost like there's an active, disruptive force working against the model's stability. It's as if these errors aren't just passive gaps in my data, but are actively interfering with the coefficients I'm trying to estimate.\n\nIs there any formal way to discuss the idea of \"unobserved agency\" or \"interference\" from external factors that aren't just passive noise, but specifically disrupt the structural integrity of the regression? I need to be able to describe this sense of \"instability\" or \"disruption\" in a way that sounds legitimate for a sociology journal.",
