      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, the concept of \"unobserved agency\" or \"interference\" from external factors can be discussed within the framework of regression analysis, although it is not a standard term in the field. When you observe a pattern in the residuals that suggests more than just random noise, it may indicate the presence of unmodeled influences or omitted variables that are affecting your dependent variable.\n\nIn formal terms, this can be referred to as \"model misspecification.\" Model misspecification occurs when the chosen model does not adequately capture the true underlying relationship between the variables. This can lead to biased estimates and incorrect inferences. The \"disruption\" you are sensing in the residuals could be a manifestation of this misspecification.\n\nTo address this issue, you can consider several approaches:\n\n1. Model Expansion: Introduce additional variables into the model that may capture the effects of the unobserved factors. This could involve including interaction terms, polynomial terms, or other transformations of the existing variables.\n\n2. Model Selection: Use information criteria (such as AIC or BIC) to compare different models and select the one that best balances fit and complexity. This can help identify a model that accounts for the observed patterns in the residuals.\n\n3",
